17592186045417] [17592186157231 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186380740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157231 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186382497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157231 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Logically Equivalent Statements" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157231 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157231 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "bc5b8688-3838-5e9e-9304-9a4c787034ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157478 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157478 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157478 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157478 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157478 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157478 #edb/ref 1045 "Logical Equivalence" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157478 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186158012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157478 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157478 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "d786bc7e-7381-59dc-826e-1b785938d470" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157632 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186154034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157632 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186154037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157632 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186154040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157632 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186154043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157632 #edb/ref 1069 "q-182344" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157632 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the missing values in the following truth table:\n\n| $A$ | $B$ | $¬(A∧B)$ | $¬A∨¬B$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $T$ | $T$ | $F$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $T$ | $F$ | $T$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $F$ | $T$ | {{field-3}} | $T$ |\n| $F$ | $F$ | $T$ | $T$ |\n\nUsing your results, complete the following statement:\n\n$\\mid ≡$ {{field-4}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157632 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "10f78fd8-b244-51f6-bc65-b2ef2428fc6c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157803 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186155739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157803 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81351" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157803 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are logically equivalent?\n\n1. $X∧¬Y$\n2. $¬(Y∨¬X)$\n3. $¬Y∧(X∨X)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157803 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "39f394a7-df4a-5b3c-93b6-e97b645cd2dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158012 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158012 #edb/ref 1061 120201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158012 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c35dc71e-cbb8-5b1e-9e21-1319c9031864" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158012 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158013 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158013 #edb/ref 1061 120202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158013 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c619f370-2162-5e52-83a2-7a22517f0f26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158013 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158014 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158014 #edb/ref 1061 120205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158014 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5765c4ca-7ca3-542f-b314-a0b02c46501b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158014 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158015 #edb/ref 1061 120208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158015 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1c1388e3-4999-5448-a4d6-93e79c6a1bf2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158015 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158579 #edb/ref 1064 "Two statements are **logically equivalent** if they have identical truth values in their respective truth tables. In this lesson, we'll use truth tables to show that two complex compound statements are logically equivalent.\n\nIf two statements $P$ and $Q$ are logically equivalent, we write $$P\\equiv Q$$ where the symbol \"$\\equiv$\" means \"is logically equivalent to.\"\n\nFor example, let's use a truth table to prove the following logical equivalence:\n\n$$(A \\land \\neg A) \\lor B \\equiv B$$\n\nFirst, we construct truth tables with columns $A$ and $B$ containing all possible combinations of truth values. Then, we create another column for $\\neg A,$ which has the opposite truth values to $A.$\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n\nNow, we add a column for $A \\land \\neg A.$ Recall that $A\\land \\neg A$ is true if $A$ is true and $\\neg A$ is true and is false otherwise. In this case, $A\\land \\neg A$ is always false.\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\,$ | $\\, A \\land \\neg A \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n\nFinally, we complete the table with a column for $(A \\land \\neg A) \\lor B.$ Recall that $(A \\land \\neg A) \\lor B$ is true if $(A \\land \\neg A)$ is true, or $B$ is true, or both are true.\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\,$ | $\\, A \\land \\neg A \\,$ | $\\, (A \\land \\neg A) \\lor B \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n\nWe see that the truth values of $(A \\land \\neg A) \\lor B$ are the same as the truth values of $B.$ This proves that $(A \\land \\neg A) \\lor B$ is logically equivalent to $B.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158579 #edb/ref 1065 8260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158579 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "52050e7d-e6dd-5b8e-a1f9-56a8c00bbaa7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158579 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
